          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Exile and Return</strong><br>\nFollowing his abdication in 1814, Napoleon was exiled to the island of Elba. He escaped in 1815, returned to power for a brief period known as the Hundred Days, and was ultimately defeated at the Battle of Waterloo.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Final Exile and Death</strong><br>\nNapoleon was exiled again, this time to the remote island of Saint Helena in the South Atlantic, where he spent the last years of his life. He died there in 1821, with his health and circumstances of death the subject of historical debate.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Napoleon's Legacy</strong><br>\nNapoleon Bonaparte left a complex legacy. His administrative reforms had a lasting impact on France and many parts of Europe, but his quest for power also led to years of war and the loss of lives. Napoleon remains one of history's most studied and controversial figures.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
